WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.400
The acronym ERP stands for Enterprise Resource&nbsp;
Planning. An ERP system is a core enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.160
software and solution stack comprised of a&nbsp;
central database surrounded by multiple plug-in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.920
services and software packages, each of which is&nbsp;
tailored to solve specific enterprise problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:24.080
and address specific challenges. At the core&nbsp;
of all ERP solutions is a master database.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.840
This database houses all the records that a&nbsp;
company uses within all of its ERP solutions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.080
These records can be financial, like invoices and&nbsp;
purchase orders. They can be related to payments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.600
like accounts payable and payroll and taxes.&nbsp;
They can be related to inventory status so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.040
a company knows what its supply lines are and how&nbsp;
many of all finished goods are available in every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.840
warehouse. That core database also stores all&nbsp;
of the sales data, what customers have ordered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.400
what products as well as details and specifics on&nbsp;
the internal processes to get that product from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.240
the warehouse to the customer. But simply&nbsp;
storing the data by itself isn't useful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.160
The importance of an ERP system is to take all&nbsp;
the data that a company collects and use it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.320
and integrate it so that that company can find&nbsp;
ways to increase efficiency and save on costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:25.120
This is why there's always a data analytics piece&nbsp;
that wraps around the entirety of that core ERP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.520
database. This analytics engine is busy as it's&nbsp;
constantly creating reports on regular schedules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:38.240
and updating the tables themselves. But it also&nbsp;
has to have the ability to provide data to users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:45.760
on an ad hoc basis whenever a user has a specific&nbsp;
query that they need more information from. So for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.040
all that data to flow correctly into the database&nbsp;
and for the core functions of the ERP system to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.040
work correctly. Let's talk about the most commonly&nbsp;
implemented modules that you'll find within an ERP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:03.440
system. So that first module, that's going to be&nbsp;
your sales module, that is all of your sales data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.520
your order data, your customer data, so what&nbsp;
customers buying, what things, where it's going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.680
shipping, tracking all of the PO information on&nbsp;
how it's getting paid for. In the payment status&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.400
you've got to track all of that sales data,&nbsp;
but in order to make sales happen you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.560
got to have product. In order to have product&nbsp;
that means that you've got to have procurement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.720
So, for procurement you've got to go out there,&nbsp;
you've got to track what materials am I buying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.200
who am I buying them from, where are they&nbsp;
coming from, am I getting the best deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.640
am I doing all of the things that I need to do&nbsp;
to acquire what I need to make my product. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.440
procurement goes so much further than that, it's&nbsp;
not just what does it take to make your product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.040
it's also “are you procuring what you need in&nbsp;
order for your employees to keep up and running?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.040
Are you buying things as complex&nbsp;
as servers to go into a data farm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.440
and as simple as packages of ballpoint pens&nbsp;
so that people have something to sign with?”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.360
All of those pieces go into that procurement&nbsp;
area. And then in addition to the procurement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.480
you've got to have your production systems,&nbsp;
right. Production is incredibly important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.120
because you have to track that production from the&nbsp;
very beginning with your raw materials to the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.000
end with your finished product. And production is&nbsp;
going to track everything in between, it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.480
to do the planning, the capacity management,&nbsp;
the maintenance, the materials management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.640
being sure that the right things are being&nbsp;
staged at the right place at the right time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.960
particularly as we've moved into this&nbsp;
just in time manufacturing process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:37.520
everywhere in the world so that we get as much&nbsp;
efficiency in our manufacturing process as we can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:44.320
And so, after we finish that procurement, we might&nbsp;
have a distribution module, that module is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:50.320
to be concerned with how do we get our finished&nbsp;
products from point A to the customer's door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.440
or just from point A to the loading dock door.&nbsp;
So that distribution model that's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.080
warehouse processes and staging goods, and&nbsp;
movement of finished goods, logistics control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:08.320
inventory control, stock levels trigger&nbsp;
levels all of those things that are necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.680
from a logistics perspective because every&nbsp;
business runs with that logistics trail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.400
And so, your distribution modules have to keep&nbsp;
track of all of that. They've got to run all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.320
of it for you or you're just going to run into&nbsp;
inefficiencies. And then related to manufacturing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.160
and still very important, you've got your asset&nbsp;
pieces, right. So, you've got your assets and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.040
have to track them. These are fixed assets,&nbsp;
capital assets, you have to know everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.640
about them. Not just the procedural information,&nbsp;
what do you have, who made it, how does it work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.760
what's its status, does it have any maintenance&nbsp;
coming up, is it still under a service contract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.480
those things are important. Don't get me wrong,&nbsp;
but your finance department is going to insist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.000
that you also have all of that finance related&nbsp;
data, what's the valuation of your asset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.640
what's its depreciation status, what is it is its&nbsp;
expected useful life, should they be planning on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.520
a capital expenditure in order to replace it in a&nbsp;
certain number of years. Tracking all of that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:14.160
key to the asset. Part because, again, this fits&nbsp;
in holistically and so all of these services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:20.720
these are all the back office ones. Back office&nbsp;
is important, but also important is front office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.560
and this is the point where we should cut back&nbsp;
to me doing this very nice scripted speech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:32.880
So, let's talk about these front office customer&nbsp;
facing or customer touching solutions, things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.880
customer relationship management, software, right.&nbsp;
That's your CRM piece. Why does CRM matter? Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.800
CRM matters because that's everything to do&nbsp;
with your relationship to that customer. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:50.560
customer information, sales history, it's incoming&nbsp;
leads on people who are potentially interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.840
buying your products, it's opportunity from&nbsp;
first identification to opportunity close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.200
it's all of your sales tracking data and your&nbsp;
pipeline information that you report out on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:07.840
All of that goes into that CRM system. That data&nbsp;
is essential to knowing the status of your company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.560
and knowing what your revenue is going to be&nbsp;
as you track all of those opportunities from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.240
open to close. And then in addition to your&nbsp;
CRM suite, you've also got your HCM suite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.480
right. Human capital management, or HR software,&nbsp;
there's lots of different names for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.680
but the same general idea how are you going out&nbsp;
and sourcing new employees, how are you retaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.080
the ones that you already have, how are you being&nbsp;
sure that everybody is being judged appropriately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:38.240
that annual reviews are going well, that all of&nbsp;
the back end processes necessary to onboard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:44.080
hire and maintain and pay your employees happen.&nbsp;
All of that goes into that HCM suite. And while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.000
that might not necessarily be part of your ERP&nbsp;
system it certainly falls within the umbrella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.320
the larger category of what ERP is. And so,&nbsp;
while it might be a tightly coupled system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.240
it might also be integrated in. It really&nbsp;
just depends on which one you've picked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:07.920
And then also another tightly coupled system&nbsp;
that may or may not be part of the ERP suite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.920
is your accounting system. Now some&nbsp;
companies obviously choose to put their ERP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.920
and their accounting software together,&nbsp;
some of them choose to break it apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.360
but that accounting piece that is always going&nbsp;
to be core because the data from the accounting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.960
piece is going to feed back into everything else&nbsp;
that you're doing. Likewise, you're going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.320
finance modules these are the ones that are more&nbsp;
tightly coupled and probably built into the ERP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.920
that are going to track all of those&nbsp;
things like your company investments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.280
company’s financial status, it's going to ingest&nbsp;
all of that data we talked about before with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.480
assets and things like that. And it's going&nbsp;
to track the financial health of your company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:54.000
at a very high level. And that high level is&nbsp;
probably the most important part of your ERP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.840
system because everything that we've talked&nbsp;
about, everything on this wheel right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:04.400
this is about running the business from a very&nbsp;
operational level. But the next step is not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.280
the operations piece it's the governance piece.&nbsp;
So we're going to remove this database and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.280
going to remove these analytics engines, and we're&nbsp;
going to put governance right here in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:19.520
because every module that we have, every one&nbsp;
of these modules has to feed that data into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:26.240
holistic governance picture because executives&nbsp;
at the boardroom level, or one step down from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.960
the boardroom level, these executives are going&nbsp;
to make the decisions that they have to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:37.120
about the future of the company. These are the&nbsp;
strategic level decisions based on inputs from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:43.360
all of the modules within your ERP system. It's&nbsp;
how you look at the company health holistically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:49.520
and that's very different because it's a roll up&nbsp;
of all of the data from all of the modules that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:55.040
are in that, that are feeding into that database.&nbsp;
And so, as we bring all of this data in we create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.920
this roll-up reporting, we create this summary&nbsp;
reporting, we put that into dashboards, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:05.680
so that companies can go out and the C-suite can&nbsp;
look at a dashboard every morning that's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:12.000
show them the holistic health of the company from&nbsp;
every factory they had, every location they have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.720
every distribution point, what's going on&nbsp;
with shipping, what's going on with retail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:24.240
what's going on with procurement, all of that data&nbsp;
feeds into the ERP, and it gives the executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.920
involved the ability to do those strategic&nbsp;
level decisions. It's incredibly important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:36.960
all in all. Hopefully I've given you a good&nbsp;
overview of what an ERP system is and why it's so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:42.880
critical to the successful operation of any modern&nbsp;
company. Your ERP system is always going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:48.640
core to your daily operation and because of that&nbsp;
selecting the right cloud provider for running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:56.720
your ERP system is also critical. Your business is&nbsp;
24/7, 365, and so must be the cloud on which you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:02.560
run it. So if you'd like to talk more about how&nbsp;
to implement a new ERP system from the ground up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:09.920
or how to transition your existing ERP system into&nbsp;
a modern one, you can learn more at ibm.com cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.040
SAP, or you can reach out to your IBM&nbsp;
Sales team, or your IBM Business Partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:23.120
and we'd love to engage with you on how to improve&nbsp;
your whole company by improving your ERP system.

